The reference / pointer pick-mode kit
description: portion cells in the pure grammar, one query-cell kit and one hint seam over them
- >
  A `*` reference is entered as **portion cells** - the key-value gesture repeated. The
  decomposition lives in the PURE editor, not in the server client: `grammar/portions.ts` spells
  raw <-> portions both ways (the scope ladder, quoted keys, `[n]` folding), the dispatch table's
  `portion` cell owns the grammar (`:` splits or climbs the scope, `[` folds an index, Backspace
  merges or descends, Enter joins and parses-or-refuses), and the cursor's `RefEntry` carries the
  committed cells - wholly cursor-level, the document holds the OLD pointer or nothing until the
  full reference parses. The debug editor gets the same pointer entrance with no server anywhere.
- >
  **Completion hints are a separate, optional service** (`complete.ts`): a `HintProvider` answers
  "what could the active portion be" (the query carries the ladder, the committed portions, the
  live prefix, the container path, the document and the mount's server addresses) and the cells
  draw the dropdown. THE DOCTRINE: hints ADVISE and never validate or gate typing - the grammar
  works identically with no provider at all, and a picked hint only replaces the active cell's
  text (committing stays the grammar's Enter). The pure editor ships `docHints` (the in-memory
  document - bare and document scopes only); the server host plugs the live tree in instead.
- >
  The server host is `renderers/yed-cells.tsx`: `treeHints` is the server's `HintProvider` -
  the bare scope's holder is the cursor's container spelled onto the wire (`serverPathOf`,
  `yed-sync.ts` - the same addressing law the ops use), the committed portions join to the
  context query and `treeCandidateProvider` (`client/query-complete.ts`) answers with the
  context's real children over `GET /api/query` (plus the query grammar's operator rows).
  `makeSourceCells()` only adds the idle affordances now (the go-to-target arrow, the
  `$yamloverLink` descend); the entry and retarget faces ARE the pure portion cells. The
  breadcrumb and the tag picker (`renderers/annotate.tsx`) still run the query-cell kit
  (`client/query-cells.tsx` over `breadcrumb-machine`) - [one machine](::server:query-editor)
  in two hosts.
- >
  **The TOC is re-plugged onto the portion cells HOST-SIDE** (`renderers/yed-toc-pick.ts`
  `useTocRefPick`, mounted by `yed-editor.tsx`): a live reference edit claims the shared TOC
  filter session for its lifetime, the joined query feeds `GET /api/query?shape=filter` at the
  HOLDER (the same addressing law `treeHints` uses - the TOC prunes as you type), and a TOC row
  click spells the picked path INTO the cells (`pointer-spell`, the typed scope ladder honored) -
  INSERTED, never committed: the grammar's Enter stays the one commit, exactly like a dropdown
  hint. The pure editor remains TOC-BLIND - the seam rides public surfaces only (EditorState in,
  the pure `refFromRaw`/`refRawOf`, setState out); with no session in context the hook is inert,
  and eviction (another host claimed the TOC) only unplugs the panel - the edit stands.
- >
  Commits ride yed's PURE grammar (commitPending - the same Enter path typing takes: the
  sibling rule, the refusal ring, the watchdog; a REFUSED commit keeps the portion cursor and
  the typed text stands), and the wire spelling is COMPACT (`yed-sync.ts payloadOf` respells
  via `renderPointer({spaced: false})` - the server's single-line `*\S*` payload gate). Outer
  states: [pointer_entry](::server:yamlover-editor:pointer_entry) /
  [pointer_pick_editing](::server:yamlover-editor:pointer_pick_editing) /
  [atom_focused](::server:yamlover-editor:atom_focused).
